## Why This Is Asked
Introduces access control in a CNPA stack, a common beginner-friendly area with real-world implications.

## Key Concepts
- RBAC, middleware, JWT validation
- Log redaction and PII protection
- Unit/integration tests, basic load testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Express-style middleware sketch
function rbac(requiredRoles){
  return (req,res,next)=>{
    const token=req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
    const payload=verifyJwt(token); // assume helper
    if(!payload || !requiredRoles.includes(payload.role)) return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
    req.user=payload; next();
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support multi-tenant roles?
- How would you test for token revocation and short-lived tokens?

## Why This Is Asked
In CNPA stacks, authentication rotation can create bottlenecks that affect all requests; this question evaluates your ability to diagnose cross-service latency and implement effective auth caching strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed tracing across HTTP, database, Kafka, and Redis services
- JWKS caching and cache staleness trade-offs
- Resource pooling and batch processing optimization
- Canary validation and metrics-driven verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// JWKS cache example
const jwksCache = new Map();
async function getKeys(kid){
  const cached = jwksCache.get(kid);
  if (cached && !isExpired(cached)) {
    return cached.keys;
  }
  const keys = await fetchJWKS();
  jwksCache.set(kid, { keys, timestamp: Date.now() });
  return keys;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests triage of a mixed HTTP, MongoDB, Vault path and teaches safe caching of per-request secrets, a common beginner pitfall.

## Key Concepts

- Latency breakdown and instrumentation
- Vault per-request secret calls vs caching
- MongoDB indexing and query patterns
- Safe TTL-based cache and rotation

## Code Example

```javascript
class VaultCache {
  constructor(fetchFn, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.fetchFn = fetchFn;
    this.ttl = ttlMs;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }
  async get(key) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const cached = this.cache.get(key);
    if (cached && now - cached.ts < this.ttl) return cached.value;
    const value = await this.fetchFn(key);
    this.cache.set(key, { value, ts: now });
    return value;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cache invalidation after Vault rotation?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure no stale secrets are used during load?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about end-to-end exactly-once semantics across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and Redis in CNPA stacks, including failure modes and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once across services
- Outbox pattern and transactional boundaries
- Deduplication using client_request_id
- Idempotent sinks/consumers and cache invalidation
- Observability: duplicates, latency, retry rates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: HTTP handler scaffold
async function handleOrder(req, res) {
  const id = req.headers['client_request_id'];
  if (!id) return res.status(400).send('missing id');
  await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO orders (...) VALUES (...);');
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO dedupe (client_request_id) VALUES (?) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;', [id]);
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO outbox (order_id, event) VALUES (...);');
  });
  res.status(200).send({ status: 'accepted' });
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate idempotent consumer behavior under replay scenarios?
- What metrics and alarms would you configure to detect dedupe or latency anomalies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical handling of cross-region failover, monotonic reads, and coupling between database, messaging, and cache layers in a CNPA stack.

## Key Concepts
- MongoDB transactions across shards/replicas
- Read/Write concerns for consistency
- Outbox pattern for reliable Kafka publishing
- Redis cache invalidation strategies
- Observability: replication lag, read-after-write latency, cache metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-node example using MongoDB driver
const session = client.startSession();
try {
  await session.withTransaction(async () => {
    await orders.insertOne(order, { session });
    await outbox.insertOne({
      aggregateId: order._id,
      event: 'ORDER_CREATED',
      payload: { /* ... */ },
      processed: false
    }, { session });
  }, { readConcern: { level: 'snapshot' }, writeConcern: { w: 'majority' } });
} finally {
  await session.endSession();
}
```

